first question just curious how this  whole alphabet situation has changed  your job and your approach at Google  Ventures well thanks I'm really good too  it's really business as usual for us  we've always operated independently made  our own investment decisions and invest  it apart from Google's product roadmap  so not much has changed for us so you  had a role in the formation of Calico  which is Google's efforts to extend our  lifespan what are some concrete examples  of progress there and opportunities you  see to really extend our lives I think  the opportunity in the life sciences is  really hard to understate at this point  in time I think the change that we're  gonna see between now and the next 20  years is going to be similar to what  we've seen happen in communications over  the last 20 years where you can talk to  anyone anywhere anytime by video  essentially for free and 20 years ago  you would write a letter and that's a  pretty big change and I think we're  gonna see a similar change enabled by  computational capabilities over the next  20 years in healthcare and I'm really  excited about it  so let's talk about investment  opportunities I mean give us some  specifics what are the most interesting  and exciting opportunities you see out  there right now why we look at the  healthcare ecosystem as a as a continuum  so so there's Diagnostics there's  therapeutics  there's payer systems there's devices so  there's a lot of different areas in  healthcare and when you apply machine  learning and and new technology to each  of those areas there's some pretty  exciting opportunities and just to give  you one example  Eddy toss is a company we've invested in  in the gene editing space which is  something that would have been hard to  even imagine 15 years ago as a company  but it holds great promise to hopefully  treat a number of diseases that are  really untreatable right now now I know  this idea of immortality is very  fascinating to  I was recently speaking with Sebastian  throng the founder of Google X about the  same thing and he said that he thinks  that we will significantly be able to  extend our lives in our lifetime and and  the question is is really why don't  humans live to two hundred years I mean  how far along are are we in that process  bill I mean are we really gonna see an  additional 10 20 years added to our  lives in our lifetime what was really  careful to talk about that because when  we say in our lifetime none of us really  know what our lifetime is you know I  could I could say yes and walk outside  and get hit by a bus I mean these things  happen but I think in a reasonable  amount of time I think we can see real  improvements in treating a number of  diseases and I think to talk about  immortality is a is a good way to kind  of take it to its extreme but we're  talking about the realities of science  and and I think when we think about that  we have to think about the fact that we  have the ability to extend life right  now so if we just a lot of people  talking about the redistribution of  wealth and I like to think about the  redistribution of health and so when I  think about that the average lifespan in  the u.s. is something like 70 to 80  years but in western Africa it's about  40 we have the ability right now with  our existing technology to extend life  it's just a matter of do we have the  commitment to really see it through and  globally and not just in this country  now I want to talk about your opinion on  on fairness and Elizabeth Holmes you  recently told Business Insider we looked  at their notes a couple of times but  there was so much hand waving like look  over here that you know we just couldn't  figure it out so we had someone from our  life science investment team go into  Walgreens take the test and it wasn't  that difficult for anyone to determine  that things may not be what they seemed  here now she responded to you at the  Wall Street Journal conference saying  he's never met with us he's never  reached out to us if he just wants to  talk badly about us he's free to talk  badly about us it's a free country what  really happened  well I think the first part of what you  said is exactly true we had someone from  our team go and take the test and  instead of one vial of water one nano  container of blood to turn into five  with the follow-up call several days  later looking for even more blood and it  just was apparent that if things were  not what they seems and and I think it's  really important because when you're  dealing in the life sciences you're  dealing with a really vulnerable  population of people patients that are  banking on some of the promises of this  new technology so I have to be really  careful about the claims that you make  and I think in this case what's been  written about the company and what's  been what is being written I think  speaks for itself  you know obviously if if what she's  saying is possible really is possible  that's amazing but it is really scary to  me that a company could raise money at a  ten billion dollar valuation if there  really isn't that much there I mean bill  what do you think actually happened here  do you think maybe they just got too far  ahead of themselves you know I read a  really interesting Cora answer to this  question just yesterday and I think what  happened I think there's a lot of  culpability and a lot of the media has  something to do with it getting carried  away with a story and not really looking  into the science I mean we've got life  science companies that we invest in  23andme is a great example they've  they've published over 40 peer review  articles on their science and no one's  ever questioned the accuracy of the data  that they're providing to consumers and  in this case with any of the data and  that's part of the problem and whatever  we would ask the media would ask  Elizabeth Holmes about the the data and  the technology they would say it was a  trade secret which makes me wonder why  so many magazines put her on the cover  of magnet put her on the cover and she's  on that board kind of on the board of  Fellows of Harvard Medical School from  what I understand and and  I don't know that any of those folks  have seen the science behind it because  there hasn't been a single peer-reviewed  journal article about it  now you're also an investor in in  23andme which just saw a pretty major  comeback after its own run-ins with the  FDA  I just yesterday sat down with Anne  Wojcicki and she talked about how far  they've come  take a listen I feel great like I am I'm  super proud of the company and what  we've accomplished because I think when  you have to slog through and you just  have to put your head down and know that  there's a lot of work ahead of you and  you're not gonna see a reward for a  couple years it's hard and you know we  had an incredible retention rate people  stay then people were determined and  they work through it and I think  actually even with the launch I almost  feel like that was just like we just  climbed the first flight of stairs but  we're still climbing the Empire State  Building so we just there's a lot more  to go and so for me part of what I think  about too is pacing is that we have it's  it's amazing we're back on the market we  have we have an incredible product we've  completely redesigned everything and  what I'm really excited about is that  this is the first chapter and a whole  new book for 23 me you're gonna see that  full interview on an upcoming episode of  studio 1.0 but bill how would you  compare 23m ease situation with the FDA  with what we're seeing with tharros and  what is your vision for the future of  this you know return the 23andme being  apart from the fact that they're both  companies I don't see much of a  similarity I mean 23andme has that team  has put their nose to the grindstone  over the last two years and worked  extremely hard with the FDA to get over  some regulatory hurdles to be able to  bring their product to market which is  really different from having the very  basis of the do you actually have a  product and does does it does it work  and is it accurate be questioned those  are two it's really different to try  something that's difficult and run into  roadblocks and work through them to  through to claims that you have  something and have no evidence that it  actually exists those are really  different situations now I want to talk  to you also about robotics and I  this is something else that you've had a  long-standing interest in are you still  seeing good opportunities out there or  have they all been bought by Google and  Facebook already you know I think the  question of AI and machine learning it's  it's not so much looking for general AI  companies to invest in for us that  artificial intelligence is it's all  around us it's on your phone your phone  helps you find where to go it can tell  you the weather or there there's  artificial intelligence in your car  there's there have been computer games  that can beat the greatest chess masters  in the world for many years so it's  around us it's a it's a layer on top of  a lot of the companies that we invest in  that are helping to accelerate some of  their innovations and we're seeing other  companies like uber for example become  more interested in in artificial  intelligence Google Ventures of course a  big investor in uber how much of Givi  success is predicated on that investment  and how complicated is it now that  Google obviously makes self-driving cars  and uber is working on its own  self-driving cars  why don't say our investment in uber  isn't successful yet the company is  still growing and faces hurdles and  challenges and so I'd be really careful  to say that that I don't think any  success that we might have is predicated  on the company because they've got a lot  of hard work ahead of them and we're  trying to support them in that so how  then would you describe your  relationship with uber right now given  that to some extent they're trying to  compete with Google at least on the  driverless car front and some would say  mapping you know when a company gets to  any company gets to a certain kind of  breakout size they're gonna compete with  lots of things but whether it's Google  Facebook are there companies in our  portfolio of investments and that's not  a surprise to us that that happens and  and when we first met with Travis the  the idea of a self-driving car existed  so so no one is shocked by by this  development and and you know we're  investors in uber so we're big believers  in in Travis and that team and we have  lots of friends that work there so we  really would like to see them succeed  now I would love to get your thoughts on  the environment right now are you seeing  more down round  out there where are we in this sort of  funding supercycle this is this is when  investing in startups becomes a  spectator sport  that's when I start to worry about  things and I lived through 1999 and 2000  when it started to become a real  spectator sport and I think we're kind  of into that area again and I think 99  to 2000 was a period where companies  that had no business going public we're  going public much too soon and now we're  in an era where the pendulum has swung  the other way where companies that  probably should go public or ready to go  public are waiting much longer than  maybe they should in some cases and it's  just a matter of time before the  pendulum swings back the other way as  well so what does that look like and  what are the biggest warning signs out  there that you're seeing I think it  looks like you know you have these these  billion dollar-plus valuation companies  some of which will do really well and be  the foundational companies technology  companies of the future and and some  that won't do very well because it  wasn't the right time or the people  worked hard and it didn't work out and  and because the valuations are high and  they get a lot of attention when when  they don't succeed they'll probably get  a lot of attention as well and so when  people ask the bubble question in to me  what they really mean is is someone  about to lose a lot of money and at  those valuations when companies don't  succeed there is money to be lost and  it's just it's part of the startup  lifecycle and so again none of that is  really surprising to me I just think  it's part of the business cycle that  we're in

